          %h1#the_continuum_hypothesis The Continuum Hypothesis
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#statement"} Statement
              %li
                %a{:href => "#unprovability"} Unprovability
              %li
                %a{:href => "#unrefutability"} Unrefutability
              %li
                %a{:href => "#EastonTheorem"} Generalization: Easton’s theorem
              %li
                %a{:href => "#links"} Links
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %blockquote
            %p
              Cantor’s continuum problem is simply the question: How many points are there on a straight line in Euclidean space? In other terms, the question is: How many different sets of integers do there exist? &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "#Goedel47"}> Gödel 1947 p 515
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            The
            %strong continuum hypothesis
            is a famous problem of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            concerning the cardinality of the 
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+real+numbers"} Dedekind
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            (the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum"}> continuum
            ”). The hypothesis in its classical form goes back to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Cantor"} G. Cantor
            and was on top of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert%27s+problems"} Hilbert's list
            of open problems in mathematics in 1900.
          %p
            In concise form the continuum hypothesis (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CH
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CH
            ) reads:
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ℵ
                          %mn 0
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi ℵ
                      %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \quad 2^{\aleph_0}=\aleph _1\quad
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
            of the Dedekind real numbers is either
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+set"} countable
            or has the same
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinality"} cardinality
            as the set of all Dedekind real numbers.
          %p
            The
            %em generalized continuum hypothesis
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi GCH
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GCH
            ) states more generally:
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ℵ
                          %mi k
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi ℵ
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \quad 2^{\aleph_k}=\aleph _{k+1}\quad
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GCHinZF"}
            below.)
          %p
            The
            %strong independence of the continuum hypothesis
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            axioms of set theory has been established in landmark papers by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kurt+G%C3%B6del"} K. Gödel
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+J.+Cohen"} P. J. Cohen
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ZFC
                  %mo +
                  %mi CH
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC+CH
            relative to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ZFC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC
            in 1938, and the latter proving the consistency of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ZFC
                  %mo +
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi CH
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC+\neg CH
            relative to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ZFC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC
            in 1963. For fully
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+proof"} formal proof
            see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Han18"} Han 18
          %p
            The broader implications of the independence results for set theory in general and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ZFC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC
            in particular are somewhat controversial. They are widely taken as a pointer towards the deficiency of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ZFC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC
            and the need for
            %em new axioms
            of set theory. This position has been voiced famously in
            %a{:href => "#Goedel47"} Gödel (1947)
            from a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/platonism"} platonist perspective
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"} W. Lawvere
            in
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere03"} 2003
            interpreted Cantor’s original point of view as saying that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CH
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CH
            holds for ‘sufficiently structureless’ sets and, accordingly, viewed Gödel’s 1938 result as a proof of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CH
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CH
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Patrick+Dehornoy"} P. Dehornoy
            = succeed "," do
              %em Woodin
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CH
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CH
            is actually conjectured to be false.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Solomon+Feferman"} S. Feferman
            has argued more recently that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CH
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CH
            is essentially
            %em mathematically indefinite
            and has made notions of ‘indefiniteness’ explicit that indeed enable to back this point of view with technical results (cf.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Feferman11"} Feferman (2011)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joel+David+Hamkins"} Joel David Hamkins
            has argued (cf.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Hamkins12"} Hamkins 2012
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set-theoretic+multiverse"} multiverse
            view of set theory,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CH
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CH
            is settled by our extensive knowledge of models of set theory satisfying both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CH
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CH
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi CH
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg CH
            which all seem fully set-theoretic, so that no principle implying either one can possibly be considered ‘obviously true’.
          %p
            The attempt to give categorical accounts of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forcing"} forcing
            methods introduced by Cohen provided a strong impetus in the development of (elementary)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            in the work of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Freyd"} Freyd
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Miles+Tierney"} Tierney
          %h2#statement Statement
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              be an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+topos"} elementary topos
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              %strong continuum hypothesis
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              asserts that if there is a sequence of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphisms"} monomorphisms
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi A
                    %mo ↪
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \hookrightarrow A\hookrightarrow \Omega^N
            %p
              then either there exists a monomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \hookrightarrow N
              or a monomorphism
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mi N
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^N \hookrightarrow A
          %p
            When the topos is Boolean, so the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cantor-Schroeder-Bernstein+theorem"} Cantor-Schroeder-Bernstein theorem
            holds, this implies the existence of an isomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \simeq N
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi N
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^N \simeq A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
          %p
            In the classical case (that is, in the topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            with the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinal+numbers"} cardinal numbers
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi α
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi ℕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|\mathbb{N}|} \lt \alpha\lt {|\Omega^\mathbb{N}|}
          %p which it is more common to write as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℵ
                    %mn 0
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi α
                  %mo &lt;
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℵ
                        %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \aleph_0 \lt \alpha \lt 2^{\aleph_0}
          %p
            In the formal proof outlined in (
            %a{:href => "#HanvanDoorn20"}> Han–van Doorn 20
            ), the statement is fomulated in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lean"} Lean
            as:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Ord
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi ω
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall x, Ord(x)\Rightarrow x \leq \omega \vee P(\Omega) \leq x
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\leq y
            means
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subquotient"} subquotient
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ord
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ord(x)
            is a predicate that returns True if and only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal"} ordinal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(\omega)
            in size. In the presence of classical logic and the well-ordering principle, this then implies that no set can have cardinality between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(\omega)
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutral+constructive+mathematics"} neutral constructive mathematics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ≅
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \cong L^\mathbb{N}
            between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            and any set of sequences of elements of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+object"} non-trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+lattice"} distributive sublattice
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+of+truth+values"} lattice of truth values
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \subseteq \Omega
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ≅
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \cong 2^\mathbb{N}
            between the real numbers and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cantor+space"} Cantor space
            or a bijection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ≅
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \cong \Omega^\mathbb{N}
            between the real numbers and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            (cf.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Bauer22"} Bauer 2022
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mi ℕ
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^\mathbb{N} \subseteq \mathbb{R}
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi ℕ
                    %mo ⊆
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^\mathbb{N} \subseteq L^\mathbb{N}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+real+numbers"} Cauchy real numbers
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HoTT+book+real+numbers"} HoTT book real numbers
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+real+numbers"} Dedekind real numbers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum"} continuum
            or some non-trivial distributive sublattice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            of the lattice of truth values:
          %ul
            %li
              Given a notion of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              %strong (Cauchy/Dedekind/etc…) real continuum hypothesis
              states that if there is a series of monomorphisms such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi A
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \hookrightarrow A\hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}
          %p
            then either there exists a monomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \hookrightarrow N
            or a monomorphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \hookrightarrow A
          %ul
            %li
              Given a non-trivial distributive sublattice
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \subseteq \Omega
              of the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+of+truth+values"} lattice of truth values
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi L
                        %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^\mathbb{N}
                \-continuum hypothesis
              states that if there is a series of monomorphisms such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi A
                  %mo ↪
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \hookrightarrow A\hookrightarrow L^\mathbb{N}
          %p
            then either there exists a monomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \hookrightarrow N
            or a monomorphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mi ℕ
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^\mathbb{N} \hookrightarrow A
          %p
            Note that for any notion of real number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            it is impossible for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            to be in bijection with the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            to be both
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcountable"} subcountable
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countably+indexed"} countably indexed
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^\mathbb{N}
            to be in bijection with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mi ℕ
